Below is a minimal Java‑style snippet that shows how you would invoke the `RootObjectMapper.doXContent` helper from the **functions** namespace.  
The snippet assumes you already have a `RootObjectMapper` instance called `rootMapper` and an `XContentBuilder` instance called `xContentBuilderInstance`.  

```java
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Prepare the parameters for the doXContent helper.
//     • includeDefaults : true   → serialize all default values
//     • skipRuntimeFields : true → omit runtime fields from the output
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
ArrayList<Object> params = new ArrayList<>();
params.add(Boolean.TRUE);   // includeDefaults
params.add(Boolean.TRUE);   // skipRuntimeFields

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Call the helper – this will write the mapper’s XContent into
//     the supplied XContentBuilder.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
Object result = functions.RootObjectMapper_doXContent(
    Map.of(
        "builder", xContentBuilderInstance.toString(),   // builder argument (stringified)
        "params", params.toString()                      // params argument (stringified)
    )
);

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Inspect the result (optional)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
System.out.println("Result of doXContent: " + result);
```

### What this does

| Step | Action | Why it matters |
|------|--------|----------------|
| 1️⃣ | Build a `params` list with two booleans (`true`, `true`). | These flags tell the helper to include default values (so you’ll see the default dynamic date formats, dynamic templates, date detection, numeric detection, etc.) and to skip runtime fields. |
| 2️⃣ | Pass the `xContentBuilderInstance` and `params` to `RootObjectMapper_doXContent`. | The helper serialises the `RootObjectMapper`’s configuration into the builder, honouring the flags you passed. |
| 3️⃣ | Print the returned value. | The helper returns whatever the underlying `doXContent` method returns (often the builder itself), so you can verify the operation succeeded. |

### Notes & Tips

* **Including Defaults** – Setting `includeDefaults` to `true` forces the serializer to emit *all* default values, even if they match the current configuration. That’s why you’ll see entries like `"dynamic_date_formats": ["strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis"]` even if you haven’t overridden them yourself.
* **Skipping Runtime Fields** – By passing `skipRuntimeFields=true`, the helper will omit any runtime field definitions from the output. If you want to keep them, change this flag to `false`.
* **Dynamic Templates / Date Detection / Numeric Detection** – These are part of the mapper’s internal state. As long as your `RootObjectMapper` instance has been configured (or left at its defaults), the helper will automatically serialise them when `includeDefaults=true`.

Feel free to tweak the boolean flags or add more parameters if you need finer control over the serialization process.
